# ARK Phone Must Not Regress Production Telephony

**Effective immediately.** Production Poly VVX desk phones (Twilio **Programmable Voice SIP domain** — not Elastic SIP Trunking) and Twilio Programmable Voice webhooks are **production-critical infrastructure**.

ARK Phone development **must never** destabilize production voice transport while chasing mobile Client SDK work.

## Non-Negotiable Rule

ARK Phone success is measured **in addition to** the existing phone system—not by changing it.

If a change risks breaking:

- Poly VVX registration to the Twilio Programmable Voice SIP domain
- Twilio TwiML ring groups and Programmable Voice webhooks
- Transfers, hold, voicemail on the Twilio path
- Existing advisor workflows

…the change **must not deploy**.

## Production Telephony Is the Baseline

Current VVX-on–Programmable Voice SIP domain deployment is the **reference implementation**. ARK Phone (Twilio Client SDK) adapts to the same CallSession authority and ring policy.

**Not Elastic SIP Trunking.** That product was the Asterisk/PBX trunk path and is retired.

Do **not** reintroduce shop PBX ingress or parallel voice transports.

If ARK Phone cannot connect while VVX phones ring, default assumption:

> **The problem is in the mobile Client SDK integration until proven otherwise.**

## Engineering Principle: VVX First

When mobile fails, ask:

> **Would the VVX succeed under the exact same conditions?**

| Answer | Action |
| --- | --- |
| **Yes** | Burden of proof is on the mobile client. Compare against working VVX behavior before touching server config. |
| **No** | Investigate the server — with VVX certification as the regression gate. |

This mindset prevents chasing server-side ghosts on every mobile registration or lifecycle issue.

## Production Telephony Certification

**Lock the certification process, not just the code.**

Before any production telephony change is merged or deployed, the following must pass.

The shop runs on VVX phones today. ARK Phone replaces them over time—but until the last Poly phone is unplugged, **the VVX system is the production standard.**

### VVX Certification (required — deployment gate)

- Incoming call to every ring group
- Outbound call
- Hold
- Blind transfer
- Attended transfer (if currently supported)
- Voicemail
- Intercom/paging (if used)
- Existing call flows unchanged

**If VVX certification fails, deployment stops immediately — regardless of ARK Phone status.**

### ARK Phone Certification (required for mobile-telephony changes)

- Registration
- Outbound call
- Inbound call (if supported by current phase)
- Hold
- Transfer (if implemented in current phase)

ARK Phone certification does not override a failed VVX certification.

## Allowed Production Changes

Only when they:

- Fix **verified** bugs
- Improve reliability for **all** endpoints
- Add capabilities **without** changing existing behavior
- Are backwards compatible with every deployed VVX phone

## Prohibited During ARK Phone Development

Do not repeatedly change without concrete server-side evidence:

- PJSIP transports, global WebRTC config, endpoint templates
- Authentication model, dialplan, ring groups, SIP/TLS transports
- Traefik routing

Changing production infrastructure to "try something" is **not acceptable**.

## Debugging Strategy

When mobile SIP fails:

1. Verify production VVX phones still work (run VVX certification)
2. Verify Asterisk still works
3. Ask: **Would the VVX succeed under the exact same conditions?**
4. Compare ARK Phone against a working VVX endpoint
5. Isolate the mobile client
6. Fix the mobile client

Do **not** begin by modifying production telephony.

## Product Rule

A working Poly VVX phone is the **certification baseline**.

ARK Phone is complete only when it behaves like a VVX endpoint **without requiring production telephony to be continuously reconfigured.**

The objective is to **replace** the desk phone—not destabilize it.

